---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Code from '../../components/Code.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

const pkg = SITE.hostingPackageName;
const install = `npm install --global ${pkg}
# or, without installing: npx -p ${pkg} viewer-upload …`;
const hostingCmd = `npm install --global firebase-tools
firebase login

viewer-upload hosting chair.glb chair.usdz --project your-project --site yourname-models --dry-run
viewer-upload hosting chair.glb chair.usdz --project your-project --site yourname-models`;
const storageCmd = `gcloud auth login

viewer-upload storage chair.glb chair.usdz \\
  --bucket your-project.firebasestorage.app \\
  --origin https://www.your-site.com --dry-run`;
const check = `viewer-check https://yourname-models.web.app/models/chair.glb --origin https://www.your-site.com`;
---

<Base title="Firebase hosting" description="Host your 3D models in your own Firebase project, with the viewer-upload CLI.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Hosting</p>
  <h1>Firebase</h1>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>We do not host your models.</strong> These steps put them in a Firebase project that you own and pay for (often nothing, within
      Firebase's no-cost limits). We never see your files or your login.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Which Firebase product</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data">
      <thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Plan</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Firebase Hosting</td>
          <td>Spark (no cost) or Blaze</td>
          <td>A fixed set of models. URLs like <code>https://yourname-models.web.app/models/chair.glb</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cloud Storage for Firebase</td>
          <td>Blaze only</td>
          <td>Many models, or adding them often. Since 3 February 2026, Storage needs the pay-as-you-go Blaze plan, which has a no-cost quota.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      On Blaze, set a budget alert in Google Cloud Billing. Firebase's pricing page has the current limits:
      <a href="https://firebase.google.com/pricing">firebase.google.com/pricing</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Install the helper</h2>
    <p>
      <code>viewer-upload</code> runs the Firebase CLI or the Google Cloud CLI on your computer, signed in as you. It never reads, stores or
      sends credentials itself. Every command accepts <code>--dry-run</code>, which prints what it would run and changes nothing. Node
      20.11 or later.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Code title="Terminal" code={install} />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Route 1: Firebase Hosting (Spark plan)</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Create a project at <a href="https://console.firebase.google.com">console.firebase.google.com</a>.</li>
      <li>
        In <strong>Hosting</strong>, add a site just for models (Add another site), for example <code>yourname-models</code>. If you already
        host a website in this project, use the separate site: each deploy replaces everything on the site it deploys to.
      </li>
      <li>Install the Firebase CLI and sign in, then upload:</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <Code title="Terminal" code={hostingCmd} />
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The tool copies the files into <code>.viewer-hosting/public/models/</code>, writes a <code>firebase.json</code> that sets the
      Content-Type, CORS and Cache-Control headers, and runs <code>firebase deploy --only hosting</code>. Keep the
      <code>.viewer-hosting</code> folder and run later uploads from the same place, or files from earlier uploads will be removed from
      the site.
    </p>
    <p>
      CORS defaults to any site (<code>*</code>). To allow only your site, add <code>--origin https://www.your-site.com</code>, once per
      address.
    </p>

    <h2>Route 2: Cloud Storage (Blaze plan)</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Upgrade the project to Blaze, open <strong>Storage</strong> in the Firebase console, and create the default bucket.</li>
      <li>Install the <a href="https://cloud.google.com/sdk/docs/install">Google Cloud CLI</a> and sign in, then upload:</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <Code title="Terminal" code={storageCmd} />
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Remove <code>--dry-run</code> to run it. The tool:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>sets the bucket's CORS rules to allow GET and HEAD from your site (skip with <code>--skip-cors</code>);</li>
      <li>uploads each file with the right Content-Type and Cache-Control;</li>
      <li>adds a Firebase download token, so the printed URL works without changing your Storage security rules.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Anyone who has a download URL can download that file. That is true of every model on a public website, because the visitor's browser
      has to download it to show it.
    </p>

    <h2>Check the URL</h2>
  </div>
  <Code title="Terminal" code={check} />
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Or use the <a href="/hosting/own-server/#checker">browser checker</a>. Then paste the URL into your platform's snippet.</p>

    <h2>Setting it up by hand</h2>
    <p>
      The package's <code>templates/</code> folder has a <code>cors.json</code> for
      <code>gcloud storage buckets update gs://BUCKET --cors-file=cors.json</code> and a <code>firebase.json</code> with the Hosting
      headers. The fixes on the <a href="/hosting/own-server/#fixes">own server</a> page include both.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
